---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Container from '../components/Container.astro'
---

<BaseLayout title="About | Jeremy Hoover">
  <Container>
    <section class="about">
      <h1>A BIT ABOUT ME</h1>
      <p>
        As great as it is having a solid remote work setup, I do also enjoy stepping away from the
        screen. I’m a dad to two special boys and married to a sweetheart of a lady. I have a lot of
        fun playing acoustic guitar, growing vegetables in my backyard garden and hiking the trails
        around my city.
      </p>

      <h2>Principles and values</h2>
      <p>
        Some of the core guiding principles I have in work and life are <strong
          >open-mindedness, positivity</strong
        >
        and <strong>clarity</strong>.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        “The world makes much less sense than you think. The coherence comes mostly from the way
        your mind works.”
        <cite
          ><a
            href="https://www.goodreads.com/quotes/1195672-the-world-makes-much-less-sense-than-you-think-the"
            target="_blank">Daniel Kahneman</a
          ></cite
        >
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        When it comes to being open, it’s important to be aware of speculation, we have learned a
        lot about cognitive biases since research like what was popularized in the book <em
          >Thinking Fast and Slow</em
        >. This is a trait that is easier to see in others than ones self, I would appreciate it if
        you call me out on it!
      </p>
      <p>
        On positivity, I learned an important lesson from a leader in my life who made it a trait to
        never say a negative word. Even if he was feeling tired, he would push and emphasize how he
        was looking forward to a good rest that night. I make it a habit to strive to push back
        against cynicism, sarcasm, and speaking negatively. If I wouldn’t say something in front of
        you, I wouldn’t want to say it when you aren’t there. This just makes life a whole lot
        simpler.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        “Simplicity is never a goal: It is a byproduct of a good idea and modest expectations.”
        <cite
          ><a
            href="https://www.paulrand.design/life/books-articles/articles/print/2002-graphic-design-reader.html#:~:text=Simplicity%20is%20never%20a%20goal:%20It%20is%20a%20byproduct%20of%20a%20good%20idea%20and%20modest%20expectations."
            target="_blank">Paul Rand</a
          >, <small
            ><a
              href="https://www.goodreads.com/quotes/730110-simplicity-is-not-the-goal-it-is-the-by-product-of"
              target="_blank">alternate version</a
            ></small
          ></cite
        >
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        For clarity, as important as it is to strive to make communication and product presentation
        simple, it's important to get to that place with respect for users. It's easy to tuck
        elements away into menus on the path to reducing cognitive load, but getting to the point of
        something truly simple and usable needs to be done in a respectful way that avoids anxiety
        and even brings joy. I hope that the things I make can bring a smile to someone's day.
      </p>

      <h2>A little back story</h2>
      <p>
        When I was in my late teens, done highschool and ready for the world, I was at a point where
        I was not yet sure what I would want to do with my life. I worked at a local store, when
        morning, a couple of managers were working on the store layout, strategizing of how to face
        products and position items to make the best experience for customers. A light went off
        inside. I went straight to the highschool guidance counsellor and told her that I wanted to
        pursue “Design”, which in ’98 she pushed me on to Graphic Design. I had a background in art,
        so it seemed to make sense. I signed up for college and after a couple years, one summer I
        took a design job and never went back.
      </p>
      <p>
        This was the late early 2000’s now, a whole new century! I was quick to move from print to
        web, it was just a much better opportunity. After several years, I decided it was time to
        “break out on my own”, quit the day job and work freelance from my basement full time. This
        lasted 6 months, my first son came into the world and it was time for me to go back to
        office work. I took a job at a small, but quite talented marketing company, where I got to <a
          href="https://www.compasscreative.ca"
          target="_blank">work</a
        >
        <a href="https://reinink.ca" target="_blank">with</a>
        <a href="https://onefiftythreestudio.com" target="_blank">some</a>
        <a href="https://capturestudio.ca" target="_blank">great</a>
        <a href="https://jasonbouwman.com" target="_blank">people</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Today, I work as a front-end developer at a growing <a
          href="https://agencyanalytics.com"
          target="_blank">Toronto based SaaS company</a
        >. I haven’t worked on the marketing website for a couple versions, but my work is focused
        on helping new customers quickly learn the product and see it’s value.
      </p>

      <h2>Where to next?</h2>
      <p>
        When I consider the world at large, I am left with a lot of gratitude and appreciative of
        the journey. I have always considered my work to be more about the people than the things,
        and more about the help that I can give than the artifact delivered. Wherever I go, I want
        to go with full ambition, as author Bob Goff says in his book <em>Undistracted</em>,
        “ambition attracts opportunity like catnip.”
      </p>
      <p>
        I‘m always interested in talking about new opportunities, if you would like to work on a
        project together, please <a href="/contact">get in touch</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Container>
</BaseLayout>
